Keep serving when httpd cannot start the process for a connection.
On Windows, the server starts a new process for each connection, and
died with bb_perror_msg_and_die() if that failed. Since the exit status
had been set to 0 for the serving loop, one failure ended the server
with a successful status, while on other systems a failed fork() only
drops that connection.

Report the error, close the connection and go on.

---
 networking/httpd.c |    2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/networking/httpd.c
+++ b/networking/httpd.c
@@ -3284,7 +3284,7 @@
 		argv_copy[4] = itoa(n);
 		if ((foreground ?
 				spawn(argv_copy) : mingw_spawn_detach(argv_copy)) == -1)
-			bb_perror_msg_and_die("can't execute 'httpd'");
+			bb_perror_msg("can't execute 'httpd'");
 
 		/* parent, or spawn failed */
 		close(n);
